audits.verdictRationale:
**What's worth keeping.** The capture primitives are real and several are well made:
- passive native request observation, whose view-tag and bridgeless emit path I verified is wired correctly;
- the injected DOM, fetch/XHR and history hooks and the visible-video scoring;
- the strict bridge sanitizer, the SSRF-safe URL guard, the bounded MIME probe, and the tiny, correct cookie bridge.

**What's broken.** The orchestration between capture and candidate loses data silently in at least six independent ways, all confirmed by reading the code:
- engine epoch desync after reload or Desktop-site toggle;
- the rate limiter starving mutation batches, with the `seen` map blocking retries;
- the async probe overwriting the store from a stale snapshot;
- the JSON unescape being a no-op, which corrupts Instagram/Facebook URLs (confirmed by evaluating the generated JS);
- page identity that ignores the query string, so YouTube-style pages keep stale detections and a new `?v=` URL is never loaded;
- Referer-only ownership of service-worker traffic.

**Why it's hard to fix in place.** The logic is split across about 7K lines in scope plus several thousand in services, general-media and social. It has five overlapping classifiers in three languages, about 700 lines of diagnostics, and three sources of truth for page ownership (epoch, `boundAt`, generation). Those parallel ownership mechanisms are exactly where the bugs sit.

**What it can't do even when fixed.** It cannot reach goal #1 for the formats the owner cares about most: Instagram/Facebook reels (separate-track DASH served as byte ranges), YouTube adaptive streams, Reddit DASH, and any blob/MSE player whose muxed URL is never visible on the network.

**Recommendation.** Keep the primitives and delete the dead UI and verify-only code. Rewrite the JS pipeline as a small per-tab reducer. Move injection to document-start in all frames, parse JSON API responses, and add a Media3-based mux step.

overEngineering.description:
Several overlapping mechanisms decide which page a candidate belongs to:
- navigationEpoch, held both in the per-tab ref and as a copy in the engine and store;
- scope boundAt;
- general pageGeneration and social contextGeneration;
- a candidate observation window keyed by tab|epoch|generation|platform;
- isSameDocumentUrl, isSameGeneralContentNavigation and isSameOriginSpaTransition;
- the active_video/active_iframe_player evidence pipeline.

Most of the real bugs (epoch desync, SPA race, stale resurrection) come from these sources of truth disagreeing. A per-document nonce generated by the injected script, plus a per-tab reducer, would replace all of it.

audits.endToEndTrace:
HAPPY PATH (browser → finished file)
1. store/downloads/actions.ts:724 create(): upserts the SQLite catalog row (771), then upsertItem, then downloadEngine.enqueue (797).
2. manager.ts:766 enqueue():
   - reads the AsyncStorage record and upserts it as QUEUED (838-863; requiresEphemeralSession is set when cookies/session auth are present);
   - moves execution to QUEUED (866-869);
   - reads cookies through the CookieManager bridge to fingerprint the session (871-886);
   - stores session meta in memory only (897; download-session-meta.ts:55);
   - calls scheduler.enqueue with a secret-stripped context (905).
3. admission-scheduler.ts:173 enqueue → requestDrain → runDrain (316) → network gate (337) → peekFirstEligiblePending → hooks.onAdmit = manager.admitWorker.
4. manager.ts:2749 admitWorker:
   - pre-checks locks/workers/record status (2763-2820), but only before its awaits;
   - await coordinator.beginExecution (2823): a no-op, because android-controller.ts:79-82 cannot find the native module, so NO foreground service ever runs;
   - optional social URL refresh (2872-2883);
   - new TransferWorker (2893); emits STARTING;
   - fire-and-forget worker.run (2934-2959); the finally block releases the slot and runs afterWorkerSettled.
5. worker.ts:207 run():
   - resolves Paths.document/VidoraXDownloads/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.part (242-247) and runs the disk precheck, which reserves only remaining bytes + 4MB (250-262);
   - recordBase('transferring') (592);
   - resolveEphemeralHeadersForTarget (599);
   - probeSourceIdentity: an extra HEAD, with a GET fallback (626);
   - routing: multi-range (708-803; extra Range probe in eligibility.ts:205), append-range when pauseState.resumeData is set (856-881), fetchSingleStreamTransfer when the source is social or headers are present (906-927, the dominant path), otherwise native DownloadTask (929-937).
6. Dominant path, fetch-single-stream-transfer.ts:
   - deletes .part (64);
   - fetch uses a timeout signal whose link to the worker's AbortController is REMOVED right after headers (244-270; transfer-timeouts.ts:84-90);
   - each chunk: synchronous sink.writeBytes (140), then onProgress → worker.handleNativeProgress (667) → publishProgress (311) → manager.publishSnapshot (3255) → bind-engine-to-store.ts:185 → store.setTransferSnapshot.
   FRAGILE: pause/cancel/stall aborts no longer reach the body reader. With OkHttp readTimeout(0) (OkHttpClientProvider.kt:52-54), a silent network stall hangs reader.read() forever.
7. worker.ts:1296 complete():
   - emits a finalizing snapshot;
   - validateFinalDownloadFile (finalize-download.ts:42) → verifyDownloadedMediaContent reads only a 256KB prefix for files over 1MB with requireStandaloneMp4 (media-validation.ts:34-45).
   BREAKS: a faststart MP4 whose moov is larger than that window classifies as UNKNOWN (mp4-box-classify.ts:206-212) → mp4_structure_unproven → FINAL_FILE_INVALID → fail(wipePartial) deletes the whole download (worker.ts:1383-1408).
   - Otherwise commitPartialToFinalFile runs a synchronous JS copy of the entire file (file-paths.ts:82-116,178-212). BREAKS: the UI freezes for large files, and the copy needs 2x disk; if it fails, the partial is deleted (finalize-download.ts:88-96).
   - applyCompletedFileIdentity rename (1442) → upsert record complete (1488) → emit completed → store COMPLETED plus the library bridge.

PAUSE
store.pause → manager.pause (1140) → runPause (1167) → worker.pause (1221):
- sets pauseRequested and aborts the controller (this does not reach the body on the dominant path);
- awaitPauseSettle waits up to 6s (929-948);
- commitPauseAfterTransportStop (975) upserts PAUSED and emits.
The JS loop only notices the pause when the next chunk arrives.

RESUME
manager.runResume (1335):
- reads the on-disk .part size (1438-1442);
- resolveResumeStrategyFromRecord (1496);
- RANGE_RESUME fakes pauseState.resumeData = disk size (1573-1584);
- persists QUEUED (1669) → scheduler.enqueue (1745) → admitWorker → worker.run append-range (856).
BREAKS: append-range-transfer.ts writes into .rangepart and deletes it on pause or error (429-433, 442-444), so every byte fetched since the last resume is lost on the next pause or process kill.

FAILURE / RETRY
worker.fail (1542) persists FAILED and emits → admitWorker finally → afterWorkerSettled (3170) → scheduleAutoRetry (2451, setTimeout) → executeScheduledRetry (2529) → prepareRetryPartial (2610) → scheduler.enqueue.
BREAKS: stall and first-byte watchdog failures never reach fail(). StallWatchdog.fireStall aborts the controller before throwing (stall-watchdog.ts:361-378 → worker.ts:422-436), and worker.ts:1086-1112 treats an aborted signal as an external cancel and returns silently. The row sits at DOWNLOADING and nothing re-enqueues it.

BACKGROUND / PROCESS DEATH
There is no service, so the transfer lives only as long as the RN process and Doze network access.
On restart, app-initializer.ts:174 → manager.recover (1971) → applyRecoveryForRecord (2127): transferring + partial → RECOVER_TO_PAUSED, with auto-resume only if pauseState.resumeData exists (2300-2310).
Session meta is gone, so runResume throws SESSION_CONTEXT_LOST for cookie-bound jobs (1518-1528). retry() rejects non-FAILED rows (1871-1881), leaving the row stranded as PAUSED.
